ement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on node1, node2, node3 with pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered VLAN/routing/connectivity state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Each node shows a pre-reboot boot_id, a reboot invocation, connection loss during the reboot window (timeouts/reset errors), a different post-reboot boot_id, and confirmed recovery of VLAN interfaces/routes and successful cross-VLAN ping. This is cross-checked by the fixed lifecycle snapshots, where before_prepare and after_executor boot-identity values match the executor's own before/after observations for all three nodes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-346e9bbf46f24f07\",\"cmd-af53f038a93446b9\",\"cmd-1c8b8df41ead4981\",\"cmd-439b975d8ba14ae3\",\"cmd-397fe31c5e5c488a\",\"cmd-d197868146194b74\",\"cmd-e69f894767c34a2d\",\"cmd-0dd6a0d56d754f47\",\"cmd-65db30d872a84342\",\"cmd-4e93ffd776a24fab\",\"cmd-213c96c030b24c91\",\"cmd-66fcf28c82884e2b\",\"cmd-932b6869f3d24f49\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:before_prepare:node3:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.82,\"summary\":\"Reviewing the chronological executor commands, all state-changing actions (VyOS VLAN sub-interface configuration, forward-filter policy, NetworkManager VLAN connection creation, static-route additions to fix the metric conflict, and the three reboots) each have a clear causal link to satisfying the task or the mandatory restart protocol. No destructive, broad, or security-weakening actions were taken beyond a necessary firewall forward default-action accept required to permit routed traffic, which is a reasonable and bounded change given no other security constraint was specified. The only unnecessary residue is minor: a background tcpdump diagnostic on node1 wrote /tmp/tcpdump.log and /tmp/tcpdump.pid (cmd-95ea638778c04e00) that were not cleaned up, and the nohup-based reboot commands on node2/node3 left /tmp/reboot.log behind (cmd-e69f894767c34a2d, cmd-213c96c030b24c91), visible as added temporary-files in the after_executor snapshot. These are small, low-risk, non-destructive leftovers rather than abandoned services or broken package state; no failed services or conflicting configuration were observed pre- or post-executor.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-95ea638778c04e00\",\"cmd-e69f894767c34a2d\",\"cmd-213c96c030b24c91\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor successfully segmented node2 (VLAN 10) and node3 (VLAN 20) onto separate 802.1Q VLANs over the shared trunk link, with node1 (VyOS) routing between them via eth1.10/eth1.20 sub-interfaces. An initial cross-VLAN ping failure caused by a default-route metric conflict was correctly diagnosed and fixed with explicit static routes, and subsequent bidirectional pings succeeded. tcpdump evidence on node1's shared interface directly confirms genuine 802.1Q tagging per VLAN rather than coincidental routing. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all three nodes with clear baseline/reboot/changed-boot-id/recovery evidence, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. Minor, low-risk temporary file residue (tcpdump and reboot logs) was left behind but does not affect functional correctness.\",\"limitations\":[\"Restart/recovery verification relied on ping and interface/route checks rather than an independent, fully out-of-band confirmation of the VyOS commit persistence (though 'save' was reported and forwarding sysctls were re-confirmed post-reboot).\",\"Firewall forward default-action accept was applied broadly rather than a scoped rule; this was not evaluated against any explicit security requirement since none was stated in the task contract.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"node2 placed on VLAN 10 and node3 placed on VLAN 20 over the shared link","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 configured with NetworkManager VLAN connection 'vlan10' on enp8s0.10 (10.10.10.11/24, VLAN ID 10) and node3 with 'vlan20' on enp8s0.20 (10.10.20.12/24, VLAN ID 20), both confirmed active via nmcli/ip output.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ac66f03185a1452c","cmd-22261d51d7534125","cmd-ecaf9d185bb54837","cmd-2f528408eca9490c"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"node1 routes between the two VLANs (VyOS acting as inter-VLAN router)","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 configured with 802.1Q sub-interfaces eth1.10 (10.10.10.254/24) and eth1.20 (10.10.20.254/24), IPv4 forwarding confirmed enabled globally and on eth1, and a firewall forward default-action accept was committed and saved so routed traffic is not dropped.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1eada93ec1264d02","cmd-18fdbe67675d410e","cmd-dc9a9136ee0d465f"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"node2 and node3 can still reach each other (across VLANs, via node1)","status":"satisfied","summary":"Initial pings failed due to a default-route metric conflict (cmd-ecaf9d185bb54837, cmd-2f528408eca9490c); executor added static routes with never-default and subsequently observed successful bidirectional pings (0% loss, ttl=63 confirming a routed hop) both immediately after the fix and again after each node's restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0f5ab6bff362412b","cmd-877cadffa65c462a","cmd-0f1ea18fc6ec4890","cmd-492e553ff84f421b","cmd-65db30d872a84342","cmd-932b6869f3d24f49"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Segmentation is genuine (traffic on the shared link is actually 802.1Q tagged per VLAN, not just routed by coincidence)","status":"satisfied","summary":"tcpdump captured on 